C1 — ONE-LOCK scenario+revision+write; LoadContextWithRevision atomic. MET.
  Read internal/services/retirement/settings.go:823-847:
    823 func (sm *SettingsManager) SaveWithRevisionIfScenario(settings, expectedScenario string, expectedRevision int) (int, error) {
    824   sm.mu.Lock()
    825   defer sm.mu.Unlock()
    827   if expectedScenario != "" {
    828     if expectedScenario != sm.filename { ... &ScenarioConflictError ... }
    834     if expectedRevision != sm.revision { ... &ScenarioConflictError ... }
    840   }
    842   if err := sm.saveInternalAndBump(settings); err != nil { ... }
    846   return sm.revision, nil
  Single Lock at 824 with a deferred Unlock; both comparisons (828, 834) and
  the write (842) are inside it. No unlock/relock between check and write.
  Both mismatch branches return before saveInternalAndBump -> nothing written.
  LoadContextWithRevision (settings.go:500-535) reads settings AND revision
  under the same acquisition on every path:
    cache hit  505 RLock / 507 rev := sm.revision / 508 Clone / 509 RUnlock
    cache miss 514 Lock (defer Unlock) / 519-521 double-check returns
               sm.revision under the same lock / 524 loadInternalContext /
               529 sm.cache = settings / 534 return cloned, sm.revision
  There is no `LoadContext()` followed by a separate `Revision()` anywhere in
  the preview path: `grep -n "Revision()" internal/handlers/whatif/sync.go`
  returns no hits; sync.go:334 is
  `settings, expectedRevision, err := retirementMgr.LoadContextWithRevision(r.Context())`.

C2 — revision-bump completeness across ALL settings writers. MET (with one
  documented, harmless non-bumping write; see below).
  `grep -rn "saveInternal(" --include=*.go .` -> only 3 production hits:
    settings.go:879 (the definition), settings.go:871 (inside
    saveInternalAndBump, which bumps at 874), settings.go:679.
  `grep -rn "saveInternalAndBump(" --include=*.go .` -> 27 hits, all in
  settings.go: 788 (SaveWithRevision), 842 (SaveWithRevisionIfScenario), 932,
  960, 1001, 1034, 1065, 1084, 1115, 1143, 1183, 1216 (income/expense CRUD),
  1238 (UpdateSettings), 1304 (ApplyOverrides), 1330
  (UpdateSettingsWithPersons), 1547, 1566, 1590, 1648, 1667, 1695, 1735, 1768,
  1787, 1812 (spending phases / healthcare / big-ticket / one-time),
  2016 (CreateScenario). saveInternalAndBump ALWAYS calls bumpLocked on success
  (settings.go:870-876).
  Non-saveInternal disk writes: `grep -rn "store.WriteFile" internal/services/
  retirement/*.go` (non-test) -> exactly 2: settings.go:911 (inside
  saveInternal) and settings.go:2136 (RenameScenario), and RenameScenario
  bumps at 2144.
  Non-save mutations of manager state also bump: InvalidateCache 544,
  reconcileActiveScenarioLocked 597, BeginExternalRewrite's end() 620 (the
  restore path's gate — internal/services/restore/restore.go:217 is its only
  caller), SwitchScenario 1972, DeleteScenario 2091, RenameScenario 2144.
  There is no way to write settings from outside the package except through
  these methods: `store` is an unexported field, and
  `grep -rn 'whatif.json"' --include=*.go internal/ cmd/ | grep -v retirement/`
  yields only handlers.go:407 (a string comparison, not a write).
  OBSERVATION (not a defect, pre-existing on master, unchanged by Z7): the ONE
  non-bumping disk write is settings.go:679 — loadInternalContext's
  migration-on-decode rewrite, deliberately un-bumping per the comment at
  866-869. It cannot defeat the guard: it runs inside LoadContext /
  LoadContextWithRevision's own held write lock (461/514), and the bytes it
  writes are derived from the bytes it just read under that same lock, so it
  can never revert a concurrent editor's value.

